// RED TEAM round 3, deny-narrowing lens (2026-08-06). Deeper variant of
// LEDGER item 32 (`pair.1()` tuple-index-call bypass), explicitly flagged
// as NOT YET ATTEMPTED in docs/LAUNCH-READINESS.md S2(c): "compound nested
// nesting one level past what was tried ... t.0.1() tuple-nested-in-tuple".
//
// Root cause read directly (checker.rs::resolve_path, ~5155-5165): for a
// FieldAccess chain, resolve_path recurses on `object` and pushes each
// `field` name, building a SEGMENTS vec whose length is however many
// `.N`/`.name` hops were chained. Item 32 showed that `check_callee_
// capabilities`'s two enforcement branches are gated `segments.len() == 1`
// (named-callee branch) and `segments.len() <= 1` (fail-closed direct-
// invoke default) -- a 2-segment path (`pair.1()`, one hop) satisfies
// NEITHER and gets zero enforcement, not even the `all` fallback.
//
// A NESTED tuple-in-tuple (`t.1.1()`, TWO numeric-field hops before the
// call) should, by the identical parser mechanism (checker.rs bug report,
// LEDGER item 32's own root-cause section: the tuple-index postfix branch
// unconditionally builds `Expr::FieldAccess` and continues, never checking
// for a trailing `(`), produce a THREE-segment path (`["t","1","1"]`) --
// which ALSO satisfies neither `== 1` nor `<= 1`, so by the same reasoning
// should ALSO receive zero enforcement. Testing whether this holds for a
// depth the parent session did not itself execute.
//
// ACCEPTANCE (if the checker were sound): REJECTED (E0507 or at minimum
// the fail-closed `all` requirement) under inferred (default) mode.
//
// CONFIRMED LIVE, both directions, against the existing
// `compiler/target/release/kryos.exe` (read-only, no rebuild):
//
//   $ kryos run tests/security/attack_deny_nested_tuple_index_call.kry
//   NESTED-TUPLE-INDEX-CALL-DENY LEAK: TOPSECRET-CLOSURE-9f8e7d6c5b4a
//   RC=0
//   $ kryos check --strict-capabilities tests/security/attack_deny_nested_tuple_index_call.kry
//   RC=0   (zero diagnostics -- not even a warning)
//
// Distinct from LEDGER item 32's own repro: item 32's root-cause section
// names ONLY the plain single-digit tuple-index postfix branch
// (kryos-parser/src/parser.rs:2094-2104, triggered by a bare `Integer`
// token). This finding reproduces through the SEPARATE "chained tuple
// access" branch added specifically to un-glue the lexer's greedy FLOAT
// read of `N.M` (parser.rs:2105-2130, triggered by a `Float` token) --
// `t.1.1` lexes as `t` `.` `1.1`(float), split back into two FieldAccess
// hops by that branch, which ALSO never checks for a trailing `(` before
// `continue`-ing the postfix loop, same defect, different code path. The
// resulting 3-segment `resolve_path` (`["t","1","1"]`) satisfies neither
// `check_callee_capabilities`'s `segments.len() == 1` named-callee gate
// nor its `segments.len() <= 1` fail-closed default (checker.rs:4848,
// 4875) -- exactly item 32's mechanism, one dispatch layer deeper.

@capabilities(fs:read)
fn make_secret_reader(path: str) -> fn() -> str {
    return || file_read(path)
}

@capabilities(fs:read)
fn main() {
    let reader = make_secret_reader("tests/security/secret_for_closure_launder.txt")
    let t = (0, (1, reader))
    deny!(fs:read) {
        let leaked = t.1.1()
        println("NESTED-TUPLE-INDEX-CALL-DENY LEAK: " + leaked)
    }
}
